  "conclusion": "As the source frequency increased, the capacitive reactance decreased: the 0.1 µF capacitor measured {{mag('p1_sweep','v(n2)',300)/(mag('p1_sweep','v(n1,n2)',300)/R1) | Ω | eng3}} at 300 Hz and {{mag('p1_sweep','v(n2)',15000)/(mag('p1_sweep','v(n1,n2)',15000)/R1) | Ω | eng3}} at 15 kHz, so more of the source voltage appeared across the resistor at high frequency. The measured reactance at 1 kHz differed from the computed value by {{abs(mag('p1_dmm','v(n2)')/(mag('p1_dmm','v(n1,n2)')/R1) - 1/(2*pi*1000*C1))/(1/(2*pi*1000*C1))*100 | % | .3f}}.\n\nFor the 6.8 kΩ and 0.01 µF circuit at 500 Hz the impedance magnitude was {{sqrt(R2**2 + (1/(2*pi*f2*C2))**2) | Ω | eng3}} and the peak current was {{meas('p2_scope_vr','VR peak')/R2 | A | eng3}}. The current led the source by {{meas('p3_phase','theta') | ° | .2f}} and the capacitor voltage lagged the source by {{abs(meas('p3_lag','phi')) | ° | .2f}}; the two angles add to 90° as expected for a series RC circuit.",
